e  that terms aren't unilaterally changed  can i turn to to the issue of crime in  the capital and uh  the recent state of knife crimes have  been six homicides in the capital in  less than a week why is this still  happening  well it's very important this  conversation because they may be  watching uh to send my condolences to  the family and friends of those six  people who've lost their lives in london  they're all different uh so you can't  give one  answer to these six disparate uh awful  awful uh cases the police have made  arrests and charges in in in five of  them we have seen uh and it's no  consolation of the victims of crime or  brief families a reduction in gun crime  knife crime knife injury knife injury of  those blind age 25 in the last year  we've seen a reduction of more than 68  in teenage homicides but we are seeing  uh this summer what we see i'm afraid in  previous summers which is an increase in  violent crime the police are working  incredibly hard with the limited  resources they have they've seen police  officer cuts over the last 12 13 years  21 000 officers cut over the last 12  years the governments are going to  replace them with 20 000 offices but in  london we ask for 6 000 we've only been  given 4  300 but we are from city hall supporting  our police with 1300 more officers at  the same time we're supporting  communities giving young people  constructive things to do particularly  during the summer holidays when schools  return after schools at weekends because  we're going to make sure we deal with  the causes of crime as well as crime  itself  well yes i'm sure a lot of people hear  your call for more police officers but  but you talk about  this happening in the summer you talk  about the need for more occupation for  young people during school holidays and  they they may  think that you're blaming the weather  for the rise in violence is that what  you're  suggesting  what i'm saying is if you look back in  previous summers in august with school  holidays  uh with the other issues that we face  with a cost-living crisis without  excusing criminality we know there are  complex causes deprivation inequality  lack opportunities those have been  exacerbated by the pandemic but also by  the cost of living crisis and before the  summer days began the police myself and  others did you know explain to londoners  and others we were concerned about an  increase in violence this summer that's  why we're investing in record sums in  keeping young people busy this uh summer  more than 2 500 schemes this summer for  young people to be doing to keep them  busy thankfully we've not seen the  teenage homicides or the teenage  violence we've seen in previous summers  i'm afraid though we have seen the last  week six families bereave suffering  bereavements uh tragedies because of  these awful awful uh homicides we can't  pretend  uh that cuts haven't got consequences  and one of the consequences of cuts in  our police cuts in our public services  youth clubs youth workers youth services  has been since 2013 an increase in  serious youth violence  well yes and you say um  awful for the families who are caught up  with this but also people who live in  those areas  quite frightened  by those situations what would you say  to reassure people who are worried about  their safety  i i fully understand  people's fear of crime perception is how  they feel and they feel scared that's  why it's really important that what the  police have done is to make sure they're  freeing up  resources to have high visibility  policing uh on top of the neighborhood  teams on top of the violent suppression  units on top of the violent crime task  force we're funding from  city hall but let's not pretend  we have the number of police officers we  need in our capital city we are a  capital city with all the complexities  that brings that's why it's really  important whether it's list trust or  rishi  uh they give us the police officers